F1 (MEDIUM): the post deadline is not bounded by the run's own limit, so a slow run can still be killed between a post and its record, which this card says it ends. evidenceJobRun sets deadline = max(t0 + (limit - 20) s, tPost + 10 s). The child may run to limit - 15 s after tState, and tState is about 5 to 8 s after t0 (gh check, store cards, deploy read), so the floor sets the deadline past the limit. After the deadline, a post under way can still take FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS (8 s), then the live read (8 s) and the late postWrites (no deadline, 8 s). Probe (the job, mocked clock, 5 s before the child, 1 s posts): child 155 s ends the job at 172 s against 170; the real watchdog starts before t0. When part 1's numbers revert (limit 90, child timeout 75 s), the floor binds for any child over about 55 s; today's child is 50 to 60 s. The deadline test uses 25 s posts and never checks that the run ends before the limit. Fix: cap the deadline at the run start + limit - (FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS + the live and late tail + a margin), and give the late writes that deadline or skip them past it. Add a test that ends the child late (limit - 15 s) and asserts the job finishes under the limit.

Asked: can skipping an unchanged card hide a change? No. readingHash drops only the top-level at, and the store's other time checks (STALE_AFTER_MS on stuck, ship and e2e; agents and board freshness) are on keys posted every run. staleCopies allows an hour against a 10-minute repost. writeEvidence's rework and history come from changes between readings, so an identical reading adds nothing. A 422 or failed key is not kept, so it goes again next run. Only cosmetic: a quiet card's as-of age on the page now reads up to 10 min.

